::ENG2_U04_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] A team is publishing a digital flyer for students about lunch pre-order forms. Which element is most necessary for a clear multimedia publication?{
=A. A focused message, accurate evidence, and a visual that helps students understand the point.#Correct. A multimedia publication should combine text, evidence, and visual support for the intended audience.
~B. Several unrelated images and very little text.#Incorrect. Visuals must support the message instead of replacing it.
~C. A complete copied research report.#Incorrect. A flyer should select and adapt evidence for the audience.
~D. A message written only for adults.#Incorrect. The assigned audience is students.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence is best revised for a student audience?{
~A. Students must comply with institutional meal-planning procedures.#Incorrect. This is formal and distant for a student flyer.
=B. When students complete the pre-order form, staff can prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This is clear, accurate, and audience-appropriate.
~C. Food logistics are a problem of operational concern.#Incorrect. This is too abstract for the purpose.
~D. Meal allocation systems require procedural alignment.#Incorrect. This wording is unnecessarily complex.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual choice best supports a flyer about reducing unwanted trays?{
~A. A photo of a school hallway.#Incorrect. The image does not connect to the lunch-form message.
~B. A decorative border with no evidence.#Incorrect. Decoration does not help the audience understand the finding.
=C. A labeled chart showing fewer unwanted trays after the pre-order pilot.#Correct. The chart directly supports the central research finding.
~D. A random clip art image of a notebook.#Incorrect. This visual is unrelated to the topic.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which presentation plan best shows collaborative preparation?{
~A. One speaker handles every part because it is faster.#Incorrect. Collaboration requires shared responsibility.
~B. Each speaker gives a different unsupported opinion.#Incorrect. The presentation should stay focused on the evidence-based plan.
~C. Speakers repeat the same point without roles.#Incorrect. Repetition does not show purposeful collaboration.
=D. One speaker explains audience, one explains evidence, one explains visual design, and one explains revision choices.#Correct. Clear roles help the group present a unified multimedia plan.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which wording best combines evidence and voice for an expository publication?{
=A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after students used pre-order forms, which shows how one small routine can reduce waste.#Correct. The sentence integrates evidence and explains its significance in a clear voice.
~B. The forms were awesome and fixed everything.#Incorrect. This is exaggerated and informal.
~C. There was a report and some trays.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not explain the evidence.
~D. Waste is bad, obviously.#Incorrect. This is opinion without source support.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] A team wants its flyer to be easy to scan. Which design decision best supports that goal?{
~A. Use one long paragraph with no headings.#Incorrect. A long block of text is difficult to scan.
=B. Use a short headline, brief evidence statement, labeled visual, and clear call to action.#Correct. These elements help students quickly understand the message.
~C. Use tiny text so more information fits.#Incorrect. Tiny text makes the publication harder to read.
~D. Use five different unrelated fonts.#Incorrect. Too many fonts distract from the message.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision most improves audience awareness?{
~A. "Pre-order compliance is administratively beneficial."#Incorrect. This is too formal and unclear for students.
~B. "The food thing works if people do it."#Incorrect. This is too vague and informal.
=C. "Filling out the form helps the cafeteria prepare meals students actually choose."#Correct. This revision explains the benefit in clear student-friendly language.
~D. "Procedural forms must be submitted by all."#Incorrect. This wording is formal and rule-focused.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which statement best explains why a visual should be included?{
~A. A visual should be included because every assignment needs a picture.#Incorrect. Visuals should have a purpose, not just be added automatically.
=B. A visual should make the research finding easier to understand for the intended audience.#Correct. The visual must support comprehension of the message.
~C. A visual should replace all written explanation.#Incorrect. Multimedia publication uses visual and written elements together.
~D. A visual should be unrelated so it looks interesting.#Incorrect. Unrelated visuals weaken the publication.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] A group member disagrees with the chosen visual. Which response best supports productive collaboration?{
=A. Ask the member to explain how another visual might better support the audience and evidence.#Correct. This keeps the discussion focused on purpose and evidence.
~B. Ignore the concern and submit the first design.#Incorrect. Ignoring feedback weakens collaboration.
~C. Remove all visuals to avoid disagreement.#Incorrect. The assignment requires multimedia publication.
~D. Let the loudest member decide without discussion.#Incorrect. Collaboration requires reasoned decision-making.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains the significance of the evidence?{
~A. The evidence is there because the report had information.#Incorrect. This does not explain significance.
=B. The finding matters because it shows that student choices can help staff prepare fewer unwanted meals.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters to the audience and purpose.
~C. The report is probably useful.#Incorrect. This is uncertain and vague.
~D. Forms are used in schools.#Incorrect. This fact does not explain the lesson's research finding.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which publication choice would most likely confuse the student audience?{
~A. A clear headline about completing the pre-order form.#Incorrect. A clear headline supports understanding.
~B. A labeled chart connected to the research finding.#Incorrect. This helps the audience interpret evidence.
=C. A technical paragraph full of cafeteria management terms without explanation.#Correct. Unexplained technical language can confuse the intended audience.
~D. A short action step telling students what to do next.#Incorrect. A clear action step supports the purpose.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision keeps the meaning but improves clarity?{
~A. "The form situation was handled."#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. "Pre-ordering impacts operational tray outcomes."#Incorrect. This is unnecessarily complex.
~C. "Do it because forms matter."#Incorrect. This does not explain the benefit.
=D. "Pre-ordering helps staff prepare meals based on what students choose."#Correct. This revision is clear and keeps the original meaning.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral explanation best supports the team's visual choice?{
=A. "We chose a before-and-after chart because it directly shows the change in unwanted trays after the pilot."#Correct. This explanation connects the visual to evidence and purpose.
~B. "We chose the chart because it looked cool."#Incorrect. This does not explain how the visual supports the message.
~C. "We did not think about the visual much."#Incorrect. The publication should use purposeful design.
~D. "The visual does not really connect, but it fills space."#Incorrect. A visual should not be filler.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which detail should be removed from a student flyer?{
~A. A short explanation of why the form matters.#Incorrect. This supports the purpose.
=B. An unrelated paragraph about school parking rules.#Correct. Unrelated information distracts from the publication's message.
~C. A labeled visual showing the research finding.#Incorrect. This supports understanding.
~D. A clear student action step.#Incorrect. This helps the audience know what to do.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence uses an appropriate academic voice for the flyer?{
~A. The lunch form thing is kind of useful or whatever.#Incorrect. This is informal and vague.
~B. Students are the only reason waste happens.#Incorrect. This is blaming and unsupported.
=C. Completing the form can help the cafeteria prepare meals that better match student choices.#Correct. This sentence is clear, accurate, and respectful.
~D. Everyone better submit the form now.#Incorrect. This is demanding rather than informative.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which question should a team ask during revision?{
~A. Can we make the flyer longer even if students stop reading?#Incorrect. Length alone does not improve communication.
~B. Can we add unrelated details to fill space?#Incorrect. Unrelated details weaken focus.
~C. Can we use more difficult words to sound advanced?#Incorrect. Audience-aware revision values clarity.
=D. Will students understand the message, evidence, visual, and action step quickly?#Correct. This question checks audience awareness and clarity.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which choice best combines written and visual information?{
=A. A sentence explains the pilot finding, and a chart displays the change in unwanted trays.#Correct. The text and visual work together to teach the same message.
~B. A chart appears with no label or explanation.#Incorrect. The audience may not understand what the chart shows.
~C. A paragraph explains the finding while the image shows an unrelated sports logo.#Incorrect. The visual does not support the text.
~D. A decorative background replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Decoration cannot replace evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which team role is most directly responsible for checking that the flyer is accurate?{
=A. Evidence reviewer.#Correct. This role checks that claims match the source findings.
~B. Person who chooses colors only.#Incorrect. Color choice matters, but accuracy requires checking claims and evidence.
~C. Person who counts words only.#Incorrect. Word count does not confirm accuracy.
~D. Person who ignores the source.#Incorrect. Accuracy depends on using the source correctly.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence should be revised because it overstates the evidence?{
~A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after the pre-order process.#Incorrect. This accurately states the finding.
~B. Reminders can help more students complete the form.#Incorrect. This is a reasonable statement from the lesson evidence.
=C. Pre-order forms will completely eliminate all cafeteria waste forever.#Correct. This overstates what the evidence can prove.
~D. The flyer should explain how the form connects to meal preparation.#Incorrect. This is accurate and relevant.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which call to action best fits the student audience?{
~A. Fulfill institutional submission procedures immediately.#Incorrect. This is too formal and unclear.
~B. The cafeteria thanks stakeholders for compliance.#Incorrect. This is adult-centered and distant.
~C. Pre-order processes should be operationalized.#Incorrect. This is unclear for students.
=D. Complete the lunch form when it opens so staff can prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This is clear, specific, and connected to the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which item belongs in the final publication checklist?{
=A. The visual supports the evidence instead of distracting from it.#Correct. Purposeful visuals are required for effective multimedia publication.
~B. The flyer includes every fact the team found.#Incorrect. The team should select the most relevant evidence.
~C. The design hides the source finding.#Incorrect. The evidence should be easy to find.
~D. The message changes depending on who reads it.#Incorrect. The message should stay focused and clear.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which source integration is strongest?{
~A. A report says things happened.#Incorrect. This is too vague to support the message.
=B. The pilot report found that unwanted trays decreased after staff prepared meals from student selections.#Correct. This sentence clearly identifies the source and finding.
~C. People talked about lunch a lot.#Incorrect. This does not integrate research evidence.
~D. It was good because it was good.#Incorrect. This is circular and unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which group explanation best shows the connection between purpose and audience?{
~A. We made the flyer because the assignment said to.#Incorrect. This does not explain audience or purpose.
~B. We used random images so students would notice them.#Incorrect. Attention alone is not enough if the images do not support the message.
=C. We wanted students to understand that completing the form can help reduce unwanted trays, so we used simple wording and a clear chart.#Correct. This connects audience, purpose, wording, and visual design.
~D. We wrote it in the longest way possible.#Incorrect. Long wording may reduce clarity.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence needs revision because it is not audience-aware?{
~A. Completing the form helps staff prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Incorrect. This is student-friendly and clear.
~B. A short reminder can help students remember to submit the form.#Incorrect. This is clear and relevant.
~C. The flyer should explain the benefit in simple language.#Incorrect. This shows audience awareness.
=D. Stakeholders must enact compliance with food-service optimization protocols.#Correct. This language is too formal and unclear for the student audience.
}

::ENG2_U04_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final product best meets the lesson goal?{
=A. A student-facing digital flyer with a clear headline, accurate evidence, labeled visual, and specific action step.#Correct. This product combines collaboration, audience awareness, evidence, and multimedia publication.
~B. A private group note with no visual and no audience plan.#Incorrect. This does not meet the multimedia publication goal.
~C. A long copied report without revision.#Incorrect. This is not adapted for the student audience.
~D. An unrelated slideshow about another school issue.#Incorrect. This does not match the lesson focus or standards.
}
